From 530efcd66556c1174bed21a66ff4d7f8f0f821b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:22:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 185/209] feat(area): drag-to-size creation for box and sphere

Create Object -> Area -> Box and -> Sphere are now drawn, not dropped: the
press spawns the entity at the picked point, the drag sizes it live, the
release finishes and selects it, Esc deletes it again. One undo step for the
whole gesture, and a click with no drag still leaves the kind's default size
rather than a degenerate shape.

Why a creation TOOL and not CBaseObject::MouseCreateCallback, which is how
legacy objects size themselves while being placed: that virtual belongs to the
object class, and these entities are created as EditorQt's own
CEntityObjectWithComponent (the class desc carries the component GUID as its
file spec). Subclassing that object class to reach the virtual would mean the
shape can only ever live on an entity created as that class - alternative B4 of
decision 02, rejected there. A creation tool keeps the object class stock and
the component addable to any entity.

CShapeDragCreateTool subclasses the 1b polygon creation tool and reuses its
spawn, undo-bracket, select and cancel code unchanged; two hooks were added to
the base for it (GetMinPointCount, SeedShape), and the members it needs moved
from private to protected. Sizing goes through IShapeComponentEdit::SetPoint
alone, because both kinds already present their dimensions as the two-point set
the point tool edits - box = (min corner, max corner), sphere = (centre, radius
handle). So the drag reuses exactly the authoring path "Edit Shape" uses and
there is no second way to resize a shape that could disagree with it.

- Box: the drag rectangle is the XY footprint, and the box stands ON the drag
  plane rather than straddling it, because an area box is nearly always meant
  to cover the ground the user dragged over. Height is a default (2 m) and not
  a second gesture: a drag gives two dimensions, and asking for a vertical drag
  after the button is up is not what "drag to size" means to anyone.
  SetPoint is called max, min, max - three times and in that order. Each
  SetPoint keeps the other corner still and refuses to let the box collapse, so
  a drag landing entirely on one side of the box's current position would
  otherwise have its first corner clamped against the stale opposite one; after
  the second call the fixed corner is already the dragged one, so the third is
  unclamped.
- Sphere: the press picks the centre, the drag distance is the radius.

One tool class per object class, because the Create panel hands a custom
creation tool no user data (report 02): "EditTool.AreaShapeBoxCreate" and
"EditTool.AreaShapeSphereCreate". "AreaShapeBox" loses its default-size
placement in favour of the drag; "AreaShapeSphere" is new.

Two parity fixes of stage 1c ride along:

- Bounds now refresh immediately after a viewport shape edit (F frames the new
  volume without reselecting). CEntityObject::SetModified -> CalcBBox reads the
  ENTITY's local bounds, which a shape component does not contribute to (it
  owns no render slot), so CalcBBox saw no change and never reached its
  InvalidateWorldBox(): the editor kept the cached world box from the last
  selection. AreaShapeTools::SyncEditedComponent now calls InvalidateTM(0),
  which is the public route to that cache - CBaseObject::InvalidateWorldBox is
  protected and this is a plugin - and whose CEntityObject override re-pushes
  an unchanged transform, i.e. does nothing else. Legacy does the same one
  level down (CAreaBox calls InvalidateWorldBox after a size change,
  AreaBox.cpp:523).
- CShapeEditTool now leaves cleanly when its component is removed, or its
  entity deleted, while the tool is up - the inspector's remove-component
  button is one click away. Display() notices it (it runs every frame, while
  MouseCallback and OnKeyDown already refuse to act on an unresolved target),
  rolls back a half-open undo transaction, forgets the GUIDs and pops the tool.
  No dangling access: the tool has never held a pointer across a frame.

